    "evidence_text": "CD28 and ITK signals regulate autoreactive T cell trafficking\n\nActivation of self-reactive T cells and their trafficking to target tissues leads to autoimmune organ destruction.\n\nWe propose that ITK specifically licenses autoreactive T cells to enter tissues to mount destructive immune responses.\n\nHow membrane receptors initiate signal transduction upon ligand binding is a matter of intense scrutiny.\n\nAlthough it has long been speculated that TCR-CD3 may undergo a conformational change, confirmation is still lacking.\n\nThis occurs earlier than and independently of tyrosine kinase activation.\n\nT cell receptor ligation induces the formation of dynamically regulated signaling assemblies\n\nTcell antigen receptor (TCR) ligation initiates tyrosine kinase activation, signaling complex assembly, and immune synapse formation.\n\nLAT, Grb2, and Gads were transiently associated with the TCR.\n\nStrikingly, SLP-76 translocated to a perinuclear structure after clustering with the TCR.\n\nDirect observation of ligand recognition by T cells\n\nLocal changes in lipid environment of TCR microclusters regulate membrane binding by the CD3\u03b5 cytoplasmic domain\n\nHere, we show that TCR triggering by peptide--MHC complexes induces dissociation of the CD3\u03b5 cytoplasmic domain from the plasma membrane.\n\nThese changes in the lipid composition of TCR microclusters even occur when TCR signaling is blocked with a Src kinase inhibitor.\n\nBasic residues in the T-cell receptor \u03b6 cytoplasmic domain mediate membrane association and modulate signaling.\n\nThe precise mechanism of T-cell triggering following TCR ligand engagement remains elusive.\n\nMutations of the TCR\u03b6 BRS motifs that disrupt this membrane association attenuate proximal and distal responses induced by TCR engagement.\n\nThese mutations appear to alter the localization of TCR\u03b6 with respect to Lck as well as the mobility of the TCR complex.\n\nT cell receptor-proximal signals are sustained in peripheral microclusters and terminated in the central supramolecular activation cluster.\n\nCalcium signaling via TCR was blocked within 2 min by anti-MHCp treatment and 1 min by latrunculin-A treatment.\n\nTCR microclusters were also resistant to disruption by anti-MHCp and latrunculin-A treatments.\n\nOur studies reveal a role for F-actin in TCR signaling beyond microcluster formation.\n\nFull activation of the T cell receptor requires both clustering and conformational changes at CD3.\n\nThis conformational change at CD3 reverts upon ligand dissociation and is required for T cell activation.\n\nMembrane association of the CD3\u03b5 signaling domain is required for optimal T cell development and function.\n\nThe TCR:CD3 complex transduces signals that are critical for optimal T cell development and adaptive immunity.\n\nIn resting T cells, the CD3\u03b5 cytoplasmic tail associates with the plasma membrane via a proximal basic-rich stretch (BRS).\n\nAltered TCR signaling from geometrically repatterned immunological synapses.\n\nThese results are consistent with a model of the synapse in which spatial translocation of TCRs represents a direct mechanism of signal regulation.",
